    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d171">

                <pb facs="0174" n="162" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg162"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.91261/155: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-03-20T17:00:00+03:00">March 20, 1941—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6:41 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>547. My 386, February 27, 10 p.m.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d171-f1"/> The Counselor
                    of the British Embassy<anchor n="13" xml:id="d171-f2"/> called on
                        Thurston<anchor n="14" xml:id="d171-f3"/> today and advised of the intention
                    of the British Embassy to request its Foreign Office to impose upon the
                        Tass<anchor n="15" xml:id="d171-f4"/> correspondents in Great Britain
                    substantially the same discriminations and inconveniences to which the British
                    correspondents in Moscow have been subjected including among other things the
                    denial of the use of the long distance telephone other than from the central
                    telephone station, a stricter censorship of their messages as distinguished from
                    those sent by other foreign correspondents, and an intentional delay in the
                    transmission of their despatches commensurate with the delay encountered by the
                    British correspondents in Moscow. He added that it is the intention of
                        Reuter’s<anchor n="16" xml:id="d171-f5"/> to cooperate in retaliatory steps
                    against Tass. He expressed the hope that retaliatory steps might be taken
                    concurrently in the United States in the belief that the position of the British
                    and American correspondents in Moscow would be much alleviated by simultaneous
                        action.<anchor n="17" xml:id="d171-f6"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d171-f1" n="12" xml:id="d171-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d171-f2" n="13" xml:id="d171-f2-content">
                <p>Herbert Lacy Baggallay, Acting Counselor of Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d171-f3" n="14" xml:id="d171-f3-content">
                <p>Walter Thurston, Counselor of the American Embassy in the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d171-f4" n="15" xml:id="d171-f4-content">
                <p>Telegraph Agency of the Soviet Union, official communications organization of the
                    Soviet Government.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d171-f5" n="16" xml:id="d171-f5-content">
                <p>Reuters, Ltd., British independent news agency.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d171-f6" n="17" xml:id="d171-f6-content">
                <p>In telegram No. 367, March 26, the Department advised Ambassador Steinhardt that
                    it did not believe “it would be opportune just now” to take the suggested
                    action.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
